            <ArticleTitle>Temporomandibular joint disorders in patients referred for third molar extraction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>323-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1834-7819.2009.01157.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Third molar removal has been implicated as a precipitating event for temporomandibular joint disorders. The aim of this study was to determine what proportion of patients had pre-existing pain and dysfunction that could be attributed to the temporomandibular joints.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixty patients referred for third molar removal were clinically examined and a history of their presenting complaint recorded at the initial consultation visit. Patients were then diagnosed and categorized.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of the total number of patients examined, 13.3 per cent showed signs and symptoms of temporomandibular joint pain and dysfunction while a further 23.3 per cent also had symptomatic third molar teeth.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results of this study suggest that the signs of temporomandibular joint disorders are common in patients referred for third molar extractions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
